<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Archduchy of Austria</h1><p class="st-meta">1453 CE – 1804 CE · capital Vienna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Habsburg heartland on the Danube, raised to an archduchy in 1453, around which the dynasty built its great central European monarchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a> had been held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> since 1278. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV</a> forged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-maius">Privilegium Maius</a> around 1358, claiming the title of archduke. In 1453 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Frederick III</a>, himself a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a>, confirmed the forgery, giving the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> the rank of archduke and special privileges.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The archduchy, divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> below and above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>, was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where strong estates of nobles, clergy and towns met in diets. Its archdukes were usually also emperors. Much of the nobility turned Protestant in the 16th century but was forced back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> after 1620. Vienna withstood Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1526 the archduchy formed the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy</a>. In 1804 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, in which the archduchy remained a crown land until 1918; its territory now forms the federal states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/archduchy-of-austria.jpg" alt="Archduchy of Austria" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C._1450_Austrian_region.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1453 CE – 1804 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vienna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick III, Maximilian I, Maria Theresa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Austro-Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Austria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Erzherzogtum Österreich</dd></dl></aside></div>
